L174: ## 5 Experiment
L175: 
L176: In cite24†Sec. 5.1 , we introduce implementation details. In cite100†Fig. 3 , we present experimental results of OSI on SD2.1, which shows superiority in both performance and efficiency. In cite36†Sec. 5.3 , we provide detailed ablation studies. In cite40†Sec. 5.4 , we demonstrate the broad generality of OSI across diffusion models, schedulers, and cryptographic schemes.
L177: 
L178: ### 5.1 Implementation detail
L179: #### Diffusion Models
L180: We focus on text-to-image latent diffusion models and select Stable Diffusion (SD) [cite94†40 ] to conduct experiments. The majority of experiments run with SD2.1 [cite94†40 ] to align with GS [cite69†50 ], producing $512\times 512$ images from $4\times 64\times 64$ latents.
L181: To assess generality, we also conduct experiments on SDXL [cite104†38 ] for higher resolution ($1024\times 1024$ images and $4\times 128\times 128$ latents), and SD3.5 [cite105†10 ] for flow matching [cite106†30 , cite107†31 ] mechanism and DiT [cite108†37 ] backbone ($1024\times 1024$ images and $16\times 128\times 128$ latents). For all SD variants we adopt classifier-free guidance (CFG) [cite109†19 ], using a guidance scale of 7.5 during generation and disabling it during inversion (scale set to 1).
L182: #### Schedulers
L183: 
L184: Following GS [cite69†50 ], for SD2.1 and SDXL, we sample with 50-step DPM-Solver [cite110†32 ] and inverse with 50-step DDIM scheduler [cite84†43 ]. For SD3.5, we use the default Euler–discrete scheduler [cite105†10 ] with 28 steps of sampling and inversion. Note that the inversion schedulers apply only to the baselines. Our OSI inverses one step without scheduler.
L185: #### Dataset
L186: 
L187: We use the Stable Diffusion Prompts (SDP) corpus [cite111†16 ]. Following GS [cite69†50 ], the first 1k prompts are reserved for evaluation, while the remaining $\sim$72k prompts are used to synthesize the noise–image pairs described in cite18†Sec. 3.3 . To assess cross-dataset generality, we additionally evaluate on prompts from MS-COCO [cite112†29 ].
L188: #### Finetuning Setting
L189: 
L190: We finetune the OSI model for 11 epochs with a batch size of 16 using Adam [cite113†25 ] and a learning rate of $1\times 10^{-4}$. Random data augmentation (cropping, blurring, etc.) is applied with probability 50%.
L191: #### Evaluation Metrics
L192: Following GS  [cite69†50 ], we report 1) the true positive rate at a fixed false positive rate of $10^{-6}$ (TPR@FPR=1e-6; detailed in the Appendix) and 2) bit accuracy of the recovered watermark. To assess robustness, we also adopt the nine representative adversarial distortions used in GS [cite69†50 ] (see Appendix). To quantify efficiency, we measure FLOPs and the payload rate (see cite34†Sec. 5.2 ). Note that we only modify the extraction pipeline relative to prior methods [cite69†50 , cite72†15 , cite78†48 ].
L193: Therefore, image quality metrics (e.g., FID [cite114†18 ] and CLIP-Score [cite115†39 ]) are expected to be essentially identical and thus are not considered in our study.
L194: ### 5.2 Performance of One-step inversion (OSI)
L195: #### Performance Gain
L196: In cite116†Tab. 1 , we compare our OSI with the GS [cite69†50 ] baseline under four different $f_{hw}$ settings on SD2.1. Generally, larger $f_{hw}$ values yield higher bit accuracy but correspond to shorter embedded watermarks. Across all $f_{hw}$ configurations, OSI consistently outperforms GS, achieving higher bit accuracy and TPR in both clean and adversarial scenarios.
L197: The cross-dataset evaluations on MS-COCO [cite112†29 ] exhibit the same performance trends, as shown in cite117†Tab. 2 , indicating that the advantages of OSI are not tied to a specific training distribution and transfer robustly to a different domain.
